  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThe core idea is to evolve an optimal step function representation on an interval [0,2] while enforcing an integral of 1, by iteratively refining parent solutions and exploring higher resolutions (94 and 188 points) starting from the provided initial solution. We use gradient-based optimization with analytical gradients to directly minimize the maximum overlap, and maintain feasible parent solutions through careful interpolation and normalization. The key innovation is (1) explicit tracking of multiple parent solutions at different resolutions, (2) using a gradient-based optimizer with analytical gradients to ensure efficient steps, and (3) adaptive grid expansion to exceed metastable plateaus.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nWe represent h(x) as a step function with n = 188 points (dx = 2/188) evaluated across [0,2]. The goal is to minimize:\n    C\u2085 = max_(lag) ||\u27e8h(x)\u27e9 \u00b7 \u27e81 - h(x+lag)\u27e9||\u2081\u2096\ndefined via discrete correlation: \n    corr[lag] = \u2211\u1d62 h[i]\u00b7(1-h[i+lag]) for 188-point Fourier transform to avoid recurrence.\nThe constraints are:\n    0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i  \n    \u2211h = 94 to ensure integral 1 at dx=2/188\n\nImplementation uses analytical gradients of the relative entropy to maintain parent solution continuity and use SLSQP for constrained optimization.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n**Parent Validation**\n- Check input length is 47, else use fallback interpolation\n- Pre-normalize all parents with \u2211h=47/2\n\n**Grid 47 Base**\n- Run constrained K-means to identify sparsity pattern\n- Initialize parents over validity windows\n\n**Grid Expansion (94/188)**\n```python\ndef expand_parent(parent47, n_target=94):\n    h_new = np.interp(np.linspace(0,2,n_target), np.linspace(0,2,47), parent47)\n    norm = (n_target) * (2/n_target)\n    h_new = h_new * norm / np.sum(h_new)\n    return np.clip(h_new, 0, 1)\n```\n\n**Gradient & Objective Compute**\n- Objective: ||max corr|| with manual correlation\n- Gradient: -\u2207([-\u2211 log(h_i + \u03b5) - \u2211 log(1-h_i + \u03b5)]) preventing gradient instability\n```python\ndef obj_grad(x):\n    dx = 2/len(x)\n    corr = np.zeros(2*len(x)-1)\n    for lag in range(2*len(x)-1):\n        for i in range(len(x) - abs(lag)):\n            if i - abs(lag) >= 0:\n                corr[lag] += x[i] * (1-x[i-abs(lag)])\n    g = np.zeros_like(x)\n    for i in range(len(x)):\n        for lag in range(2*len(x)-1):\n            tmp = 0\n            if lag - i >= 0 and i + lag < len(x): tmp += x[i] * (1-x[i+lag])\n            if lag in range(-(len(x)-1), -i) or lag > (len(x)-1-i): tmp += x[i] * (1-x[i+lag])\n            d = (1 if (i+lag in valid_indices) else 0 ) - (1 if ... else 0 )\n            g[i] += dx * d\n    return g\n```\n\n4. Search Choices & Variations\n- Starts with K-means clustering to locate \"on\" regions\n- Temporal persistence: maintains 3 parent solutions (47/94/188) through each optimization iteration\n- Checks parent diversity (minimum 2 distinct parents)\n- Cross-grid mutation: interpolates multidirectionally between existing parents\n\n5. Compute Budget & Robustness\n- Grid 47: ~20 sec\n- Grid 94/188: ~430 sec\n- Fallback to parents if constraints fail\n- Time checkpoints between parents\n- Objective scaling: logs C5 values to explore decreasing trends\n\n6. Validation & Return Contract\n- Returns tuple (best_h, C5_value, sample_count)\n- Validates with: ||\u27e8h\u27e9\u2211h * dx == 1|| < 0.001\n- Computes C5 matching original correlation function\n- Returns 47-point parent initially if requested\n- Clip(x,0,1) to ensure boundedness\n- Explicit time monitoring to break/bail",
